import Mathlib

/-!
# Ryser's conjecture on circulant Hadamard matrices

Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryser's_conjecture_on_circulant_Hadamard_matrices
-- Credit: @Dugongue (agent: Claude Code, model claude-opus-5-5)

Source statement (Ryser, 1963): no real circulant Hadamard matrix has order greater than 4.
A circulant Hadamard matrix of order `n` is an `n × n` circulant matrix `H` with entries in
`{-1, 1}` such that `H Hᵀ = n Iₙ`. (The source reports an unverified claimed proof, OpenAI 2026.)

Conventions:
* the circulant matrix generated by `a : Fin n → ℝ` is Mathlib's `Matrix.circulant a`, with
  `(circulant a) i j = a (i - j)` (indices mod `n`). The source writes `H r s = a (s - r mod n)`;
  that matrix is the transpose of `circulant a`, and `H Hᵀ = n I` holds for a circulant matrix
  iff `Hᵀ H = n I` (for `n > 0` a one-sided inverse of a square matrix is two-sided),
  i.e. iff it holds for the transpose, so the conventions agree;
* entries in `{-1, 1}`: `a j = 1 ∨ a j = -1` for every `j`;
* `n I` is `(n : ℝ) • 1`;
* order greater than 4: `4 < n`.
-/

namespace OMA.S_wp_ryser_s_conjecture_on_circulant_hadamard_matrices

theorem main : ∀ n : ℕ, 4 < n → ∀ a : Fin n → ℝ, (∀ j, a j = 1 ∨ a j = -1) →
    Matrix.circulant a * (Matrix.circulant a).transpose ≠ (n : ℝ) • (1 : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) := by
  sorry

end OMA.S_wp_ryser_s_conjecture_on_circulant_hadamard_matrices
